{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Moses consented.</b> What did Moshe see that drew him to attach himself to Yitro, an idolater? Rather, Moshe was fleeing from Pharaoh and feared him, lest he fabricate accusations against him wherever he went, so that they might kill him. Therefore he went to marry into one of the priests, for they were ministers to idolatry, because all their property was free — the king had no jurisdiction over them and no claim against them, as it is written (Genesis 47:22): ״רק אדמת הכהנים לבדם לא היתה לפרעה״, and it is written: ״כי חק לכהנים מאת פרעה״. Therefore he wanted to marry into one of them and take his daughter after he had converted. And the reason he chose Yitro from among the priests was that he had many daughters, so that he would make him a bargain and accept him immediately and not scrutinize him too closely. From this it says: ״ולכהן מדין שבע בנות״ — for what need was there to mention that he had seven daughters? Rather, for this reason he wanted to attach himself to that place. And he wanted to swear an oath to them, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״ויואל משה״ — this means nothing other than an oath. Thus I have heard from my master, Rabbi Dan of blessed memory." "<b>And he gave Zipporah his daughter to Moses.</b> She is called צפורה from the word צפור, which is a pure bird, because she was destined to convert and to purify herself from the impurity of idolatry and to be joined to Moshe."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The prooftext כי חק לכהנים מאת פרעה is a partial quotation of Genesis 47:22 (״כי חק לכהנים מאת פרעה ויאכלו את טובח״); Rabbeinu Bahya does not supply the verse reference here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:25.456482053Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}